  "text": "Neither solution gives the correct formula, so I choose NONE.\n\n**Solution 1** claims Skew(2^e) = 2^{e-2}(e+1). At e = 3 this gives 8. My recollection of Conder's tables is that Skew(8) = 6, so the formula already fails at the first case in the stated range. I also can't place the attribution to Conder, Jajcay, and Tucker, so the cited source doesn't support it.\n\n**Solution 2** claims Skew(2^e) = 2^e − 2. This matches Skew(8) = 6, but its argument fails. The second family, φ_t (fixing even x and sending odd x to x + t for even t), is not a skew morphism in general. Take e = 3 and t = 2, so φ = (1 3 5 7) with the evens fixed. Testing φ(xy) = φ(x)·φ^{i_x}(y) at x = 3, y = 3 needs φ^{i}(3) = 6 for some i. But the orbit of 3 under φ consists only of odd residues, so 6 never appears. The count of 2^{e−1} for this family is therefore unjustified, and the total 2^e − 2 is not a reliable general formula. It also would give 14 at e = 4, which I have no basis to confirm.\n\nI could not check the exact published formula for Skew(2^e) with any tool, so I am working from memory. Both proposed answers fail on the evidence I can check, and the correct formula is not among them.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
